{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794769666,"nonce":"vCBWLEFww6ZX40DPBlMxY0DZR+4=","message":"Second nAIR slice, 18cf71a7: objects. The lowering now covers new, this, instance variable reads and writes (including +=), and direct calls of methods that no subclass overrides, from non-generic classes. Checked on real Haxe (crates/ash_tast/tests/objects): the lowered classes verify and compute the source's answers in the reference interpreter (135, 270, 25.0); bump's summary is exactly one class, Acc.total, named by the declaring class (the soundness rule from comment 31, item 2, now implemented in TypeBuilder::field_slot); in Acc.run's loop the load of limit stays in the loop at O2 without the summary and leaves it with the summary. No timing: this is a structural result on one loop, not a speed number. Decisions: a method that is overridden, or overrides, is refused (is_virtual_method), so no CallMethod yet; a subclass-to-base conversion is refused by coerce; only functions a body calls have to be defined, because building any class's layout also builds String and allocates function ids for its methods (lower_all no longer lowers every allocated id). Not covered: statics, arrays, strings, closures, generics, super, null, interfaces, and the nair2hl bridge has not been run on objects, so method-table entries with no lowered function are unchecked there. Next, in order of what the TAST can give that bytecode cannot: virtual calls devirtualized from the closed class hierarchy (exact receivers), then statics and strings.","files":null}]}